WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.960
Movie villains often look monstrously&nbsp;
inhuman, but in general, the actors who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.960
play them are in fact bona fide human&nbsp;
beings. Let's take a look behind all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.520
the makeup and prosthetics to get a good view&nbsp;
of the folks who bring those baddies to life.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.000
Danny DeVito makes a great villain,&nbsp;
though in our hearts he's absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.680
anything but. He unforgettably demonstrated&nbsp;
his penchant for evil in 1992's Batman Returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.480
in which he played classic Batman foe The&nbsp;
Penguin. His real name is Oswald Cobblepot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.760
which sounds a bit like a fake name&nbsp;
that DeVito's It's Always Sunny in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.240
Philadelphia character Frank&nbsp;
Reynolds might come up with.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:37.120
DeVito is a pretty physically distinct individual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.080
so plenty of his fans probably recognized him in&nbsp;
Batman Returns even underneath all the makeup.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.400
But the criminal gang leader who was raised&nbsp;
by a family of sewer penguins doesn't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.720
look anything like the actor we know and love.&nbsp;
Designing the character involved experimenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:54.160
with multiple nose shapes and many&nbsp;
different makeup hues. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.640
DeVito's face was painted a pasty white, his&nbsp;
teeth were made to look rotten, and his eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.920
were heavily shadowed. And most disgustingly&nbsp;
of all, the Penguin drools black saliva.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.840
"It would just kind of ooze&nbsp;
out, you know, and drip down."

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.600
Believe it or not, actor Doug Jones' eyes&nbsp;
aren't really located in the palms of his hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.320
like how they are when he plays the Pale Man&nbsp;
in the 2006 fantasy film Pan's Labyrinth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.360
In real life, his bodily features are where you'd&nbsp;
expect them to be, except when he's utilizing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.840
contortionist skills. But on screen, he can&nbsp;
often be seen playing otherworldly creatures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.920
If you're a film buff, you've probably seen&nbsp;
plenty of his work, but chances are you wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.640
recognize him on the street, as his roles nearly&nbsp;
always require heavy make-up and prosthetics.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.200
Jones is a very lean 6 foot 3 inches tall,&nbsp;
which makes him perfect for a wide variety of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.640
non-human roles, like Billy in Hocus Pocus or the&nbsp;
Amphibian Man in The Shape of Water. It's fitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.040
and unsurprising then that he collaborates&nbsp;
often with director Guillermodel Toro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.600
who's known for his distinct and elaborate&nbsp;
visuals. Del Toro directed Pan's Labyrinth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.080
in which Jones played both the Pale Man and the&nbsp;
Faun. In real life, Jones is a genial man who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.480
describes himself as a "dyed-in-the-wool&nbsp;
Christian from the Midwest." Adorably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:08.720
he recently recreated his engagement photo&nbsp;
with his college sweetheart-turned-wife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.680
on their 35th anniversary and&nbsp;
posted the result on Twitter.

00:02:13.318 --> 00:02:17.360
Hugo Weaving is so unrecognizable as the Red&nbsp;
Skull in Captain America: The First Avenger that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:22.320
some fans might not have even noticed that he was&nbsp;
replaced by another actor in Avengers: Infinity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.360
War and Endgame. The new Red Skull was brought&nbsp;
to life by Ross Marquand , in a part largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.640
created with CGI. Things were a lot more physical&nbsp;
for Weaving, as he sat in the makeup chair for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.840
hours on set while the design team worked their&nbsp;
magic with seven pieces of overlapping silicone .

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.000
The difference between these two Red Skulls&nbsp;
is actually what led to Weaving turning down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.760
the opportunity to return to the role.&nbsp;
As he revealed to Time Out in 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.000
Marvel backed away from the contracts that they'd&nbsp;
originally agreed upon by attempting to pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.240
Weaving less for the new movies than for&nbsp;
The First Avenger. Weaving recounted,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.640
"They said: 'It’s just a voice job, it’s not a&nbsp;
big deal.' I actually found negotiating with them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.200
through my agent impossible. And I didn’t really&nbsp;
wanna do it that much. But I would have done it."

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.200
While you probably won't see Weaving in any&nbsp;
future Avengers films, you can certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.200
see him in plenty of other high-profile&nbsp;
movies. He played the revolutionary masked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.280
main character in V for Vendetta, and his&nbsp;
real face became easily recognizable after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.440
he played Agent Smith in the Matrix movies&nbsp;
and Elrond in The Lord of the Rings trilogy.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:24.640
Jim Carrey's notoriously expressive face&nbsp;
can render him unrecognizable from one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.840
moment to the next. The versatile&nbsp;
actor has played many roles that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:33.040
have utilized his chameleonic nature. He was&nbsp;
cartoonishly transformed for 1994's The Mask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.080
he sported bright red hair and a green mask&nbsp;
as the Riddler in 1995's Batman Forever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.480
and then in 2000 he wore head-to-toe green&nbsp;
fur in How the Grinch Stole Christmas.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.960
You might call all of those roles a series of&nbsp;
fortunate events, as they made Carrey famous and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:52.320
earned him plenty of dough. But the coincidences&nbsp;
that plague the Baudelaire siblings in 2004's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.480
Lemony Snicket's A Series of Unfortunate&nbsp;
Events, in which Carrey played Count Olaf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.280
aren't nearly so whimsical. Portraying Olaf was&nbsp;
no walk in the park either, as Carrey reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:05.520
had to spend three hours in the makeup chair&nbsp;
each day to play the villain who stalks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.400
tortures the Baudelaires. But it wasn't all bad,&nbsp;
as he's admitted that he had a, quote, "childlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.960
fascination" with the transformational powers of&nbsp;
makeup that he experienced in that film. It's safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.600
to say that his fans share the fascination and&nbsp;
love him no matter what form his features take.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.360
Villainy and makeup go hand-in-hand with&nbsp;
horror movie clowns, as demonstrated clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:32.240
by Pennywise, the terrifying jester played by Bill&nbsp;
Skarsgård in both 2017's It and 2019's It Chapter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.720
Two. The Swedish actor got into his clown makeup&nbsp;
early, asking his girlfriend to prepare his face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.880
for an audition that just required some simple&nbsp;
whiteface. Of course, he ultimately landed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.000
role and fully transformed into the Pennywise we&nbsp;
know and fear. But Skarsgård's real transformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.360
occurred on the inside. He's called Pennywise his&nbsp;
most exhausting role ever, on both a physical and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:55.360
psychological level, and he wasn't even talking&nbsp;
about the two and a half hours it took to complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.600
the look. The part required an enormous amount&nbsp;
of energy from the actor, and he also had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:05.200
deal with the fact that his performance made&nbsp;
at least one child actor cry real tears.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:09.440
"I'll feast on your flesh as I feed on your fear."

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:14.480
Skarsgård comes from a family of actors, all&nbsp;
of them quite handsome. His father Stellan has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:18.240
also been physically transformed for roles&nbsp;
like Bootstrap Bill Turner in the Pirates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.240
of the Caribbean films. It's a testimony to&nbsp;
the power of makeup that you'd never look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.320
Pennywise and the barnacle-faced Bill and&nbsp;
realize they're actually father and son!

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:31.760
Darth Maul, the vermilion villain from the&nbsp;
Star Wars universe, was played by Ray Park,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:36.640
a martial artist and actor who got his start&nbsp;
as a stunt double in 1997's Mortal Kombat:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:40.880
Annihilation. Since then, he's appeared in&nbsp;
a number of action-oriented parts, with Maul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.440
being the most famous. Park loved playing him, one&nbsp;
reason being that becoming the character through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:50.400
the magic of makeup gave him a real rush. The Sith&nbsp;
Lord's appearance instantly communicates that he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:55.040
a formidable presence and a major menace. His&nbsp;
unique look, combined with Park's particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.600
set of skills, resulted in a villain who truly&nbsp;
terrified every Star Wars fan in the world. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:05.440
a 2019 interview with SyFy Wire, Park recalled a&nbsp;
directive he received from producer Rick McCallum:

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:09.360
"From Rick, it was like, 'Now, Ray, I&nbsp;
want you to be mean. I want you to scare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.960
every child in America and around&nbsp;
the world. You're gonna be bad.'"

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.120
This turned out to be an inspired casting&nbsp;
choice, as Park was able to use his martial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:21.600
arts background to battle in a lightsaber&nbsp;
fight grounded in very little script direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.760
His face might not be recognizable in&nbsp;
this role, but his moves definitely are.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:31.760
When Daveigh Chase isn't climbing out of the TV&nbsp;
as she did in the 2002 horror classic The Ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:36.800
she's a considerably less terrifying figure. In&nbsp;
fact, she's even worked as a model. Her TV and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.040
movie parts have definitely been on the darker&nbsp;
side, though. Her first major live-action role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:46.320
was the 2001 psychological thriller Donnie Darko&nbsp;
as the title character's little sister, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:52.160
starting in 2006, she played the sociopathic&nbsp;
Rhonda Volmer on the HBO series Big Love.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:57.600
In The Ring, Chase played Samara , whose fiendish&nbsp;
powers have created a cursed videotape that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.240
kills people seven days after they've watched it.&nbsp;
While Samara is mostly seen with long black hair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:07.360
a haggard face, and a white gown, we do briefly&nbsp;
get to see her without ghoulish makeup in one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:12.160
scene. During a recording of a psychiatric&nbsp;
session watched by Naomi Watts' character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:17.280
Samara discusses her inability to control her&nbsp;
psychic powers. Chase was only 12 years old when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:21.280
the film came out, so you might not recognize&nbsp;
her nowadays if you see her on the street both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.520
because she's probably not wearing all that&nbsp;
villain makeup and because she's all grown up.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.400
"I'm sorry. It won't stop."

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.840
With his performance as the Joker in 2008's&nbsp;
The Dark Knight, the late Heath Ledger gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:40.480
the world perhaps the most iconic portrayal of&nbsp;
the Clown Prince of Crime. Though he was rendered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:46.160
unrecognizable by makeup, stringy green hair, and&nbsp;
a devilish sneer, his name has become synonymous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:50.160
with the role. That's in large part because&nbsp;
Ledger didn't just become the Joker when he put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.400
on the makeup. His commitment ran much deeper&nbsp;
than that. Throughout production on the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:59.120
he kept a diary full of playing cards, quotes&nbsp;
from the movie, and excerpts from the comics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.680
that reflect the neuroses of the character.&nbsp;
He locked himself away in apartments and hotel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:09.040
rooms to work on the diary, allowing himself to&nbsp;
perfect his portrayal of the terrifying criminal.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:14.160
"See, I'm not a monster. I'm&nbsp;
just ahead of the curve."

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.000
Underneath the Joker makeup, Ledger was&nbsp;
a handsome man with dirty blonde hair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:22.560
He also had plenty of other talents and hobbies&nbsp;
besides acting, as he was both a former chess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:28.240
champion and an avid photographer. His acting&nbsp;
work ranged from the 1999 teen rom-com 10 Things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:33.520
I Hate About You to wrenching dramas like 2005's&nbsp;
Brokeback Mountain. The Joker was just one of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:37.840
many triumphs on screen, but it's one that&nbsp;
fans of his will always remember with awe.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:43.600
The greatest mystery of Rebecca Romijn's Mystique,&nbsp;
who first appears on screen in 2000's X-Men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:48.480
is how her blue makeup was able to stick to her.&nbsp;
It wasn't easy, as her first screen test required&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.440
a jaw-dropping 12 hours in the makeup chair, which&nbsp;
was cut down to a still-staggering nine for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.440 --> 00:09:00.640
second movie, 2003's X2: X-Men United, and finally&nbsp;
down to seven for 2006's X-Men: The Last Stand.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.640
That all sounds particularly grueling,&nbsp;
even by Hollywood standards. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:08.160
it seems like it would require mutant&nbsp;
powers for an actor to get through it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.720
especially when you consider that Romijn&nbsp;
had to be made-up and on set at 9:00 AM.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:16.800
As for the rest of her career, she's had plenty&nbsp;
of other roles that don't require quite so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.520
detailed preparation. You may have seen her, for&nbsp;
example, as Number One on Star Trek: Discovery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.960
or as Alexis Meade on Ugly Betty. And she's also&nbsp;
hosted Skin Wars, a competition show that centers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:31.120
around insane body painting challenges.&nbsp;
She certainly knows something about that!

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:35.360
It's not just the bald head and the&nbsp;
missing nose that make Ralph Fiennes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:39.920
so unrecognizable as Harry Potter nemesis'&nbsp;
Lord Voldemort. It's also the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:44.080
the actor is rather striking in real life.&nbsp;
And you don't have to look far for proof,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:47.760
as he's appeared in a number of films in&nbsp;
which his natural face is fully visible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:52.960
Though Voldemort's visage is decidedly different&nbsp;
from Fiennes', the transformational process wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:58.160
as complicated as you might expect. Temporary&nbsp;
tattoos, false teeth, and fake fingernails were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:03.280
all it took to turn this talented thespian into&nbsp;
the most despised villain in the wizarding world.

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:08.160
Over in the muggle world, you've probably&nbsp;
seen Fiennes in a variety of flicks. In 1993,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:13.280
he infamously played war criminal Amon Göth&nbsp;
in Schindler's List, which earned him an Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:18.400
nomination for Best Supporting Actor. Then&nbsp;
1996's The English Patient led to another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:22.320
round of accolades. And more recently, he's&nbsp;
appeared in the likes of Clash of the Titans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:27.520
The Grand Budapest Hotel, and the James Bond&nbsp;
franchise, starting with 2012's Skyfall.

